# ==============================================================================
# env.defaults — the PUBLIC half of the shipped server configuration.
# Every value in here is safe to publish: identifiers, URLs, ports, feature flags,
# tuning knobs, and the two tokens that are public BY DESIGN (see the annotations
# on INDEXNOW_KEY and AGENT_FREE_TOKEN).
#
# What grants access lives in deploy/env.secrets — same format, NOT in git. A new
# server gets both halves merged into .env by scripts/make-env.sh (which setup-all.sh
# and install-from-usb.sh call), so `npm install` still needs no manual editing.
#
# Why the split exists: this file used to carry the live tunnel token, the Cloudflare
# API token, the admin credential and the backup upload secret. That made the
# repository permanently un-publishable — and a tunnel token is not a mild leak: anyone
# holding it can point tukuk.org at their own machine, because the edge trusts the
# token, not the server.
# ==============================================================================

# ── Admin (email + passkey) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_EMAIL + ADMIN_PASSWORD are in env.secrets — together they are the bootstrap
# path to the passkey registration, so neither belongs in a publishable file.
ADMIN_SESSION_TTL_MS=28800000

# ── WebAuthn ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Tukuk-OS Admin
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=tukuk.org
WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=https://tukuk.org,https://www.tukuk.org

# ── IndexNow (traffic: Bing/Yandex/Naver/Seznam get URLs immediately) ─────
# The key is hosted publicly at https://tukuk.org/<KEY>.txt (file public/bd8f0317…txt).
# 1 = every newly published article from /admin is sent straight to IndexNow.
# Public by design — IndexNow fetches it from the site, so hiding it is not an option.
INDEXNOW_ENABLED=1
INDEXNOW_KEY=bd8f0317c5ab4cddb9a3dc62f7ec15ae

# ── Cloudflare Tunnel (TOKEN mode — recommended, no credentials file) ──────
# The token itself is in env.secrets (CF_TUNNEL_TOKEN). The ID below is only an
# identifier: it names which tunnel the routes belong to and grants nothing on its
# own, so it stays here where the docs and the audit script can read it.
# How to mint a new token: Cloudflare → Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → the tunnel
# → copy its token (or `cloudflared tunnel token <UUID>` with cert.pem).
# setup-all.sh in TOKEN mode writes config.yml (token + ingress) from the
# deploy/cloudflared/config.yml template — the tukuk.org / www / dashboard INGRESS
# is already there, so there is NO need to create a "Public Hostname" in the dashboard.
# A new server therefore needs the code (clone or USB) + the secrets file + `npm
# install` — the tunnel comes up automatically, no .json file, no GitHub auth.
# (The older named mode needs a <uuid>.json file — tunnel 168a53af... is
#  dead because its credentials were lost when the old server was deleted.)
CF_TUNNEL_ID=c1d8ea0f-b512-487b-b7e7-ad730c24da9e

# ── Cloudflare API (for wrangler: www worker + R2 snapshot) ───────────────
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (cfat_…) is in env.secrets. Token: My Profile → API Tokens →
# Create (template "Edit cloudflare Workers & Pages" + add R2 & Zone DNS permissions).
# With it present, every `npm install` auto-deploys the worker & enables the 6-hour
# snapshot timer; without it those two steps are skipped and say so.
# Measured 31 Oct 2026 with the token named `1-dasar-kebenaran-30-10-2026`: Zone READ,
# tunnel READ + token, Workers READ, R2 READ+EDIT ✓. It CANNOT edit DNS, CANNOT edit
# Workers Routes (so it cannot remove the `tukuk.org/*` → tukuk-fallback route the
# account moved in with — README §9), and cannot purge cache. The old `cfut_…` token is
# revoked with the old account — do not chase it.
# A SECOND credential now exists: `CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN` (in env.secrets) = Zone
# tukuk.org · DNS · Edit. It can list/create/update DNS records (used to add the missing
# `tchat` / `play` / `build` CNAMEs on 31 Oct 2026) but, like the first token, it has no
# Workers Routes and no tunnel-route scope, so it cannot finish the edge by itself.

# ── Tukuk Agent (/api/agent/* tool-calling proxy + build API) ───────────────
# AGENT_TOKEN = the Bearer used by the Cline extension inside Tukuk Agent to
# call https://tukuk.org/api/agent/v1. It is in env.secrets (an admin passkey session
# is also accepted). Do not put the real value back here.
# Editor origin (agent.tukuk.org) allowed to call tukuk.org via CORS.
AGENT_ORIGIN=https://agent.tukuk.org
# AGENT_FREE_TOKEN = the FREE-tier marker for Tukuk Builder (build.tukuk.org).
# This value is DELIBERATELY public: it is compiled into the browser bundle
# (tukuk-builder providers/tukuk.ts), so it cannot be a secret. Its protection is a
# strict rate limit (default 8/min/IP) + max_tokens constraints (default 16384).
# Real cost = our rotating cloud quota; this filter only prevents large-scale siphoning.
# It must NEVER equal AGENT_TOKEN. src/agent.js checks AGENT_TOKEN first, so one shared
# value hands every free Builder client the full tier: the rate limit and the max_tokens
# clamp never apply, and the cloud quota we pay for is what drains. Until 2026-10-31 the
# two WERE the same string, in three tracked files — both values are rotated, and the
# comparison test in test/secret-hygiene.test.js fails the build if a value from
# env.secrets ever reappears in a tracked file.
AGENT_FREE_TOKEN=da0860f427988f1b70706cc8c41a25dd7feec59cba975b6db755f32794539d6f
# AGENT_FREE_RATE_PER_MIN=8
# AGENT_FREE_MAX_TOKENS=16384
# LLM_CLOUD_ORDER = the provider rotation order for the builder AGENT (only
# kind:'openai' + tools-capable providers are used; gemini/cloudflare/litert are SKIPPED).
# GROQ IS BANNED (owner veto): 'groq' must NEVER appear in this list, and a stale
# GROQ_API_KEY in .env is ignored. The veto is enforced in code by LLM_DISABLED_PROVIDERS.
# VPS tukuk.org production value (2026-10-30 — free-only, groq removed):
#   LLM_CLOUD_ORDER=gemini-openai,openrouter,cloudflare,ollama-cloud
# Note: 'gemini-openai' = Google's OpenAI-compatible layer (function-calling OK, large free
# tier); ACTIVE ONLY when GEMINI_API_KEY is set (get a free key at aistudio.google.com).
# 'huggingface' (Inference Providers) returns 402 with zero credits — re-add the name to
# the order once credits/PRO are active. (The HF *model hub* is still used for storing
# our own trained models — that path is scripts/hf-model-pull.sh, unrelated to 402.)
# LLM_DISABLED_PROVIDERS=groq
#   Hard veto list (comma separated). Default already 'groq' — nothing to do. An empty
#   value lifts the veto; do not do that on tukuk.org.
# LLM_CLOUD_ORDER=gemini-openai,gemini,ollama-cloud,openrouter,xai,cloudflare,openai,litert

# ── Own models: Kaggle GPU training → Hugging Face hub → server ─────────────
# HF_TOKEN  = write token for huggingface.co (needs 'write' role) so a Kaggle kernel
#             can publish our trained model. The token travels as a KAGGLE SECRET,
#             not in this file. On the server it is only needed to DOWNLOAD
#             (public repos need nothing).
# KAGGLE_ID = Kaggle username (kaggle-gpu-burst.sh). Never put kaggle.json here.
# HF_TOKEN=
# KAGGLE_ID=

# ── Git branch used on the server ──────────────────────────────────────
GIT_BRANCH=main
# Daily auto-update (~04:30) on ALL servers: git pull → npm install →
# service + worker + snapshot refreshed in one pass. Set 0 to disable.
GIT_AUTO_UPDATE=1

# ── Local AI tier (Ollama + LiteRT-LM) — POLICY: OFF on the VPS ─────────────────
# The DEFAULT is already off (src/config.js: localFirst=false, litertHost=''), so there
# is no need to write anything here. Do NOT set BRAIN_LOCAL_FIRST=1 / LITERT_HOST
# unless the local tier is actually ENABLED (systemctl enable --now
# litert-lm / ollama after scripts/setup-litert-pilot.sh passes its benchmark).
# Reason on tukuk.org (6.8 GB, no GPU): ollama has no model (~/.ollama/models
# does not exist yet) and litert-lm was turned off after the pilot — its unit is disabled but
# .env once set both these values, which only fooled the admin
# panel ('local-first' was shown while every request went to the cloud).
# The local tier also sits at the VERY BACK of LLM_CLOUD_ORDER (slowest),
# so it has no latency benefit — only extra RAM risk.
# BRAIN_LOCAL_FIRST=1        # (enable ONLY together with the local service running)
# LITERT_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:8090/v1
# ── Tukuk Agent (editor /agent, port 8443) ──────────────────────────────────
# The tukuk-agent.service MUST be enabled+active: tukuk-os proxies
# /agent/* to it (src/agent-editor.js); if it is down, tukuk.org/agent → 502.
# The release is rsynced to /opt/tukuk-agent/release (536 MB, gitignored — an artifact).
# This unit reads EnvironmentFile=/home/tukuk/.config/tukuk-os/env for
# AGENT_TOKEN + the editor password — that file needs to be created once on the server.

# ── Mutation-route key (index & crawler) ────────────────────────────────────
# POST /crawl, /crawl/batch, /crawl/sitemap, /crawl/site, /documents,
# DELETE /documents/:id and POST /settings are guarded by requireApiKey(): if
# API_KEY is EMPTY, that middleware is fail-open and anyone on the internet could
# inject into / empty out our search index. setup-all.sh generates a random key
# and writes it to .env (the live value lives in the server's .env, NOT here), so
# a new install is safe by default. Local scripts send the X-API-Key header.
# API_KEY=< generated by scripts/setup-all.sh >

# ── Live traffic tiles + boot warm-up ───────────────────────────────────────
# Empty traffic vars = the keyless default (Google raster, flow overlay burned in).
# Keyed provider = set TRAFFIC_TILE_URL (must contain {apiKey}) + TRAFFIC_API_KEY
# + TRAFFIC_SOURCE_LABEL, then restart. A keyed template without a key is read as
# "no provider configured", never as a wall of 403s. See src/traffic-live.js.
TRAFFIC_TILE_URL=
TRAFFIC_API_KEY=
TRAFFIC_SOURCE_LABEL=
# WARMUP=0 turns off the post-restart page warm-up (src/warmup.js).
WARMUP=
WARMUP_PATHS=

# ── Off-site restore (scripts/rebuild-from-offsite.sh) ────────────────────────
# Two of the three values this step needs are here on purpose, and the reason is a
# circularity: the identity archive is only useful if a brand-new box can reach it
# without already having the old machine. BACKUP_WORKER_URL + CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
# are endpoints/identifiers, so publishing them grants nothing.
# BACKUP_UPLOAD_SECRET is the one credential in that trio, so it is in env.secrets —
# which means a fresh box needs the secrets file before it can restore from off-site.
# That is the intended trade: the secrets file is the single out-of-band object the
# operator carries (USB, or pasted once), and the repository is no longer it.
# BLANKED 31 Oct 2026: `tukuk-backup` lived in the OLD account and was not carried over,
# so the URL answered Cloudflare 1042 forever. Left empty on purpose — every upload script
# then takes the R2-direct branch (`wrangler r2 object put`), which the current token is
# allowed to do and which spends no Worker requests. Re-set it only if an upload worker is
# actually deployed in CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID below.
BACKUP_WORKER_URL=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=6134c115f7311412314b35f3ccf76166

# ── ONE tunnel: play.tukuk.org / tchat / build are routes on CF_TUNNEL_ID ────
# There is deliberately no CF_BUILD_TUNNEL_ID / CF_BUILD_TUNNEL_TOKEN any more. The
# second connector (a3af5bb4) is retired: its hostnames are routes on the one tunnel
# above, added in the Zero Trust dashboard. A key for a second tunnel is a standing
# invitation to recreate one.
